{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7EKwaWEc792QfR2AXg1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before I draft, one problem with the main claim, and a few questions.\n\n**The \"every other publisher\" line.** Your facts don't support it yet. You have one other publisher who paid $12,500, and your agent's range starts at $11,000, which is below $12,000. If the editor knows the market, they'll know the claim isn't true, and it could cost you trust in a relationship you've had for four years. It also works against you: if everyone pays $12,000+, why ask for only $11,500? What you can prove is still strong: \"another publisher paid me $12,500 last year for a book of the same length, and $11,000–$13,000 is standard for illustrators with my record.\" Do you have other quotes that would back up the broader claim? If not, I'd use the version you can prove.\n\n**Questions:**\n\n1. What's the editor's name, and what's the book called (or its working title)? How formal are your emails with them usually?\n2. Does the publisher already know about the 20,000+ sales and the state reading list, or will this be news to them? If you know which titles those were, I'll name them.\n3. If they say no to $11,500, would you still take the book at $9,000, or are you ready to turn it down? This decides how firm the email should sound.\n4. Is your agent negotiating this deal, or are you writing to the editor yourself?\n5. Are you OK with naming the other publisher, or should it stay \"another publisher\"?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":379,"cache_read_input_tokens":9525,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":379},"output_tokens":11,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_u